# Second brain

The user keeps an Obsidian vault at `~/Documents/Notes`. When the `dybrain`
MCP server is connected, feed it while working, in any repository.

- Call `capture_fleeting` when something is worth keeping beyond the session:
  a lesson learned the hard way, a decision and its reason, a non-obvious fix,
  a tool or idea worth coming back to. One idea per note: a short first line
  that names it, then the context and the repository or URL it came from.
- Reach for a finished note only when it is clearly due, and `search` first so
  it is not a duplicate: `create_permanent` for one settled idea in your own
  words, `create_literature` for a source the work leaned on (docs, article,
  paper, talk), `create_reference` for a tool, organization or term worth a
  wiki page. Give a Vietnamese `title` and an English `title_en`. When unsure,
  capture a fleeting note instead; the user promotes it at the weekly review.
- Call `append_daily` at most once per finished task, with one line on what
  was done and where it lives.
- Skip routine work and anything a commit message already records. Never
  capture a credential, customer data, or text from a file the credentials
  rules cover.
- Write note text without hard wrapping: one line per paragraph or bullet.
  Use the language of the conversation.
- Reach the vault only through the server's `search` and `read`; from another
  repository, never open its files directly.
